1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:11,672
In nineteen twenty-nine, as a palace in Istanbul was being turned into a museum, a bundle of old parchment was pulled from a forgotten corner of its library.

2
00:00:11,672 --> 00:00:17,666
Inside was a map on gazelle skin, signed by an Ottoman admiral in fifteen thirteen.

3
00:00:18,133 --> 00:00:19,760
It shows the Atlantic.

4
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,905
Spain and Africa on one side.

5
00:00:21,905 --> 00:00:23,903
South America on the other.

6
00:00:23,903 --> 00:00:28,933
Drawn only twenty-one years after Columbus first crossed that ocean.

7
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:37,052
And along its bottom edge runs a long, strange coastline that, some people say, should not be there at all.

8
00:00:37,052 --> 00:00:37,838
Antarctica.

9
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:41,199
Drawn three hundred years before anyone saw it.

10
00:00:41,666 --> 00:00:46,155
Was it copied from an ancient survey of a continent without ice?

11
00:00:46,155 --> 00:00:48,260
A photograph taken from space?

12
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:53,100
Or something far more human, and in its own way, far more impressive?

13
00:00:57,566 --> 00:01:00,693
Tonight we do what the mystery sellers never do.

14
00:01:00,693 --> 00:01:06,360
We take the map apart, piece by piece, and trace each piece back to where it came from.

15
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,077
Six pieces.

16
00:01:07,077 --> 00:01:08,966
Then we put it back together.

17
00:01:09,433 --> 00:01:11,570
First, the picture on the box.

18
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:22,900
The famous story says the Piri Reis map is impossibly accurate, and that its southern coast matches Queen Maud Land in Antarctica, as it looked before the ice.

19
00:01:23,366 --> 00:01:34,103
In nineteen sixty-six, the historian Charles Hapgood argued it was copied from maps made by a lost seafaring civilisation in the last Ice Age.

20
00:01:34,103 --> 00:01:37,733
Two years later, Erich von Däniken went further.

21
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:43,124
He said the map looked like a photograph taken from high above Cairo.

22
00:01:43,124 --> 00:01:48,191
Distorted exactly the way the Earth curves away from a camera in orbit.

23
00:01:48,191 --> 00:01:49,833
Aliens, in other words.

24
00:01:50,299 --> 00:01:52,332
And there is a famous letter.

25
00:01:52,332 --> 00:02:03,266
In nineteen sixty, a US Air Force officer wrote that the bottom of the map agreed very remarkably with a nineteen forty-nine survey under the Antarctic ice.

26
00:02:03,733 --> 00:02:05,741
That is the picture on the lid.

27
00:02:05,741 --> 00:02:10,599
Now let's open the box, and see whether the pieces inside actually make it.

28
00:02:11,066 --> 00:02:14,267
Piece one is the corner where the maker signed.

29
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:19,375
It is the only note written in Arabic, in a different hand from the others.

30
00:02:19,375 --> 00:02:20,533
Probably his own.

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:31,984
It reads: this map was drawn by Piri, son of Hajji Mehmed, nephew of Kemal Reis, in Gallipoli, in the month of Muharram, year nine hundred nineteen.

32
00:02:31,984 --> 00:02:34,433
That is spring, fifteen thirteen.

33
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:37,025
Piri Reis was a real sailor.

34
00:02:37,025 --> 00:02:44,766
As a young man he sailed with his uncle Kemal, a corsair who became one of the great Ottoman admirals.

35
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:56,000
When his uncle died, Piri went home to Gallipoli, on the Dardanelles, and began the most ambitious project of his life: a map of the whole known world.

36
00:02:56,466 --> 00:03:04,483
In fifteen seventeen, after the Ottoman conquest of Egypt, he presented it to Sultan Selim the First in Cairo.

37
00:03:04,483 --> 00:03:10,533
Then it vanished for four centuries, until that bundle turned up in Topkapı Palace.

38
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,205
Only the western third survives.

39
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:18,375
The tear cuts straight through the notes on its eastern edge, mid sentence.

40
00:03:18,375 --> 00:03:22,166
The full map was probably over a metre and a half wide.

41
00:03:22,633 --> 00:03:24,227
So piece one is solid.

42
00:03:24,227 --> 00:03:27,343
A named author, a place, a month, a patron.

43
00:03:27,343 --> 00:03:28,502
Nothing ancient.

44
00:03:28,502 --> 00:03:32,343
The real question is where his information came from.

45
00:03:32,343 --> 00:03:34,300
And unusually, he tells us.

46
00:03:34,766 --> 00:03:39,934
Piece two is a note in Piri's own words, explaining how he made the map.

47
00:03:39,934 --> 00:03:41,082
His source list.

48
00:03:41,082 --> 00:03:44,025
Mystery documentaries rarely read it out.

49
00:03:44,025 --> 00:03:44,600
We will.

50
00:03:45,066 --> 00:03:48,759
From about twenty charts and world maps, he says.

51
00:03:48,759 --> 00:03:53,281
Some drawn, he believed, in the days of Alexander the Great.

52
00:03:53,281 --> 00:03:54,412
Eight of those.

53
00:03:54,412 --> 00:03:56,221
One Arabic map of India.

54
00:03:56,221 --> 00:03:58,105
Four new Portuguese maps.

55
00:03:58,105 --> 00:04:00,366
And one map drawn by Columbus.

56
00:04:00,833 --> 00:04:06,899
His word for the old maps, jaferiye, is a bent form of the Arabic for geography.

57
00:04:06,899 --> 00:04:14,633
Most scholars read it as Ptolemy's Geography, the ancient atlas reprinted all over Renaissance Europe.

58
00:04:15,099 --> 00:04:17,808
Here the mystery industry takes a turn.

59
00:04:17,808 --> 00:04:20,030
Maps from the time of Alexander!

60
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:21,697
Proof of lost knowledge!

61
00:04:21,697 --> 00:04:25,100
But look at what Piri was doing with that phrase.

62
00:04:25,566 --> 00:04:33,699
In his later Book of the Sea, Piri says the Franks only rediscovered what Alexander's surveyors once recorded.

63
00:04:33,699 --> 00:04:37,766
A way to frame rival discoveries as an old inheritance.

64
00:04:37,766 --> 00:04:39,466
Not a technical record.

65
00:04:39,933 --> 00:04:45,485
And by reducing these maps to one scale, he says, this final form was arrived at.

66
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:48,707
That means stretching, squeezing and stitching.

67
00:04:48,707 --> 00:04:53,300
Every seam is a place where two sources met, and did not quite fit.

68
00:04:53,766 --> 00:04:58,218
So the box comes with an instruction leaflet: about twenty sources.

69
00:04:58,218 --> 00:05:04,066
Now let's check the pieces against them, starting with the most famous name on his list.

70
00:05:04,533 --> 00:05:07,181
Piece three is the top left corner.

71
00:05:07,181 --> 00:05:08,240
The Caribbean.

72
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:10,055
At first glance, a mess.

73
00:05:10,055 --> 00:05:12,476
A giant island standing upright.

74
00:05:12,476 --> 00:05:17,166
A coastline that seems to be Cuba and Central America at once.

75
00:05:17,633 --> 00:05:19,442
Piri explains it himself.

76
00:05:19,442 --> 00:05:30,366
A long note tells the story of a Genoese named Colombo, and of a Spaniard, captured by his uncle's men, who had sailed there with Columbus three times.

77
00:05:30,833 --> 00:05:33,093
Now look with Columbus in mind.

78
00:05:33,093 --> 00:05:35,426
He believed he had reached Asia.

79
00:05:35,426 --> 00:05:44,466
He thought Hispaniola was Cipangu, Marco Polo's Japan, which maps of his day drew as a tall island, standing north to south.

80
00:05:44,933 --> 00:05:48,590
And that is exactly how Hispaniola appears here.

81
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:49,199
Upright.

82
00:05:49,199 --> 00:05:49,961
Oversized.

83
00:05:49,961 --> 00:05:53,466
Wrong in precisely the way Columbus was wrong.

84
00:05:53,933 --> 00:06:01,541
Cuba is drawn as mainland, because in fourteen ninety-four Columbus made his crews swear that Cuba was Asia.

85
00:06:01,541 --> 00:06:06,966
Anyone who later denied it faced a heavy fine, and having his tongue cut out.

86
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:14,856
Names here, like Ornofay on Cuba's south coast, appear in Columbus's records and on no other map.

87
00:06:14,856 --> 00:06:20,366
The islet Alto Velo dates to his second voyage, in fourteen ninety-four.

88
00:06:20,833 --> 00:06:29,612
Map historian Gregory McIntosh concluded that this corner preserves a lost Columbus chart from around fourteen ninety-five.

89
00:06:29,612 --> 00:06:34,466
Perhaps the closest copy we have of how Columbus drew the New World.

90
00:06:34,933 --> 00:06:37,175
So the errors are the evidence.

91
00:06:37,175 --> 00:06:40,212
A satellite would never draw Cuba as Asia.

92
00:06:40,212 --> 00:06:43,899
Only a sailor who badly wanted to be in Asia would.

93
00:06:44,366 --> 00:06:46,890
Piece four is the long middle band.

94
00:06:46,890 --> 00:06:50,927
Europe and West Africa on the right, Brazil on the left.

95
00:06:50,927 --> 00:06:54,100
The part theorists call impossibly accurate.

96
00:06:54,566 --> 00:06:55,670
And it is good.

97
00:06:55,670 --> 00:07:00,968
The Azores, Canaries and Cape Verde islands sit about where they should.

98
00:07:00,968 --> 00:07:04,720
The gap between Brazil and Africa is roughly right.

99
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,400
One compass rose line follows the equator closely.

100
00:07:08,866 --> 00:07:10,754
But good is not mysterious.

101
00:07:10,754 --> 00:07:17,604
Portuguese pilots had worked down this coast for most of a century, measuring latitude by the sun.

102
00:07:17,604 --> 00:07:23,266
Portuguese charts like the Cantino map of fifteen oh two already show this shape.

103
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:26,713
There is even a fingerprint of copying.

104
00:07:26,713 --> 00:07:31,681
The African capes carry Turkish translations of Portuguese names.

105
00:07:31,681 --> 00:07:32,521
White Cape.

106
00:07:32,521 --> 00:07:33,362
Green Cape.

107
00:07:33,362 --> 00:07:34,050
Red Cape.

108
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:36,266
Piri was translating a chart.

109
00:07:36,733 --> 00:07:38,842
And the accuracy has limits.

110
00:07:38,842 --> 00:07:41,026
Rivers in Spain are confused.

111
00:07:41,026 --> 00:07:42,532
Islands are too big.

112
00:07:42,532 --> 00:07:44,566
South America is stretched.

113
00:07:44,566 --> 00:07:46,599
The Atlantic is too narrow.

114
00:07:47,066 --> 00:07:51,010
Follow Brazil south and you can still name places.

115
00:07:51,010 --> 00:07:51,799
Cabo Frio.

116
00:07:51,799 --> 00:07:55,270
Perhaps the earliest hint of Rio de Janeiro.

117
00:07:55,270 --> 00:07:59,766
Katino, likely Cananéia, about twenty-five degrees south.

118
00:08:00,233 --> 00:08:04,439
That was the edge of Portuguese knowledge in fifteen thirteen.

119
00:08:04,439 --> 00:08:06,813
And right there the trouble starts.

120
00:08:06,813 --> 00:08:11,833
The coastline turns hard east, and runs along the bottom of the parchment.

121
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:13,086
Piece five.

122
00:08:13,086 --> 00:08:14,372
The southern edge.

123
00:08:14,372 --> 00:08:16,944
The piece the whole legend hangs on.

124
00:08:16,944 --> 00:08:24,733
According to Hapgood, this is the Princess Martha Coast of Queen Maud Land, Antarctica, drawn before the ice.

125
00:08:25,199 --> 00:08:33,396
The idea began with an amateur, Captain Arlington Mallery, who redrew the map on his own grid in the nineteen fifties.

126
00:08:33,396 --> 00:08:37,633
Hapgood and his students then spent years fitting coastlines.

127
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:39,929
Then the Air Force letter.

128
00:08:39,929 --> 00:08:53,299
Lieutenant Colonel Harold Ohlmeyer wrote that the lower part agreed very remarkably with a nineteen forty-nine seismic profile across the ice, by a Norwegian, British and Swedish expedition.

129
00:08:53,766 --> 00:08:57,123
So let's test this piece like any jigsaw piece.

130
00:08:57,123 --> 00:08:59,052
Does it fit its neighbours?

131
00:08:59,052 --> 00:09:00,338
Test one: the gap.

132
00:09:00,338 --> 00:09:07,766
Between South America and Antarctica lies the Drake Passage, about a thousand kilometres of violent sea.

133
00:09:08,233 --> 00:09:11,403
On the Piri Reis map, there is no gap at all.

134
00:09:11,403 --> 00:09:15,208
The coast runs straight out of Brazil and keeps going.

135
00:09:15,208 --> 00:09:20,000
A survey precise enough to map Antarctica would not forget an ocean.

136
00:09:20,466 --> 00:09:21,791
Test two: the ice.

137
00:09:21,791 --> 00:09:28,340
Ice cores and seabed records show Antarctica has not been ice-free for millions of years.

138
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:32,608
Hapgood needed an ice-free coast a few thousand years ago.

139
00:09:32,608 --> 00:09:33,933
It did not happen.

140
00:09:34,399 --> 00:09:35,918
Test three: the letter.

141
00:09:35,918 --> 00:09:42,057
That survey measured one line across the ice, well under one percent of the coast on the map.

142
00:09:42,057 --> 00:09:45,688
And rock under ice sits lower than it would without it.

143
00:09:45,688 --> 00:09:47,933
It was comparing the wrong things.

144
00:09:48,399 --> 00:09:54,043
Later comparisons with full surveys of the rock under the ice found no match.

145
00:09:54,043 --> 00:10:02,399
McIntosh put it bluntly: the only similarity is that both lie south of the Atlantic, and run roughly east to west.

146
00:10:02,866 --> 00:10:07,157
Test four is the cruelest, because it comes from Piri himself.

147
00:10:07,157 --> 00:10:10,825
On this very coast he wrote: this country is a waste.

148
00:10:10,825 --> 00:10:12,762
Large snakes are found here.

149
00:10:12,762 --> 00:10:15,600
And these shores are said to be very hot.

150
00:10:16,066 --> 00:10:21,722
Nearby, another note credits a Portuguese ship blown south by a storm.

151
00:10:21,722 --> 00:10:22,611
Hot shores.

152
00:10:22,611 --> 00:10:23,661
Giant snakes.

153
00:10:23,661 --> 00:10:25,196
Portuguese sailors.

154
00:10:25,196 --> 00:10:28,266
That describes Brazil, not Antarctica.

155
00:10:28,733 --> 00:10:30,684
So why does the coast bend?

156
00:10:30,684 --> 00:10:32,202
First, the parchment.

157
00:10:32,202 --> 00:10:39,864
The historian Svat Soucek notes that mapmakers often turned a coastline to fit the space left on the skin.

158
00:10:39,864 --> 00:10:43,333
The edge of a hide is not the edge of the world.

159
00:10:43,799 --> 00:10:44,895
Second, theory.

160
00:10:44,895 --> 00:10:50,812
Since Ptolemy, geographers believed a great southern land must balance the north.

161
00:10:50,812 --> 00:10:51,981
Terra Australis.

162
00:10:51,981 --> 00:10:59,066
Maps for two centuries drew it, often joined to South America, long before anyone saw Antarctica.

163
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:02,054
So piece five belongs in the box.

164
00:11:02,054 --> 00:11:03,964
But it is not Antarctica.

165
00:11:03,964 --> 00:11:11,299
It is Brazil, bent along the edge of a skin, fading into a continent everyone assumed was there.

166
00:11:11,766 --> 00:11:13,959
Piece six is the boldest claim.

167
00:11:13,959 --> 00:11:23,366
Von Däniken said the map matches a projection centred on Cairo: what a camera would see far above Egypt, with the edges curving away.

168
00:11:23,833 --> 00:11:27,822
That is testable, and geologist Steven Dutch tested it.

169
00:11:27,822 --> 00:11:34,494
A real Cairo projection curves the equator and swings Spain far to the right of West Africa.

170
00:11:34,494 --> 00:11:39,933
On the Piri Reis map, the equator is straight, and Spain sits above Africa.

171
00:11:40,399 --> 00:11:43,452
His verdict on the overlay: nothing matches.

172
00:11:43,452 --> 00:11:47,823
Even a true view from space fits worse than a simple flat grid.

173
00:11:47,823 --> 00:11:51,500
Straight parallels mean a sailor's chart, not a lens.

174
00:11:51,966 --> 00:11:55,528
And a camera in orbit would show North America.

175
00:11:55,528 --> 00:12:00,302
Here, North America is essentially imaginary, and Cuba is Asia.

176
00:12:00,302 --> 00:12:04,166
A spacecraft cannot photograph Columbus's mistakes.

177
00:12:04,633 --> 00:12:06,852
One last look at the margins.

178
00:12:06,852 --> 00:12:09,147
Piri filled them with wonders.

179
00:12:09,147 --> 00:12:13,509
A saint who landed on a giant fish he took for an island.

180
00:12:13,509 --> 00:12:14,504
Headless men.

181
00:12:14,504 --> 00:12:15,728
Six-horned oxen.

182
00:12:15,728 --> 00:12:20,166
Monsters seven spans long, but, he writes, harmless souls.

183
00:12:20,633 --> 00:12:24,677
These come from old bestiaries and medieval world maps.

184
00:12:24,677 --> 00:12:27,838
Nobody claims aliens drew the headless man.

185
00:12:27,838 --> 00:12:33,500
The map mixes fresh sailors' reports with inherited legend, on the same skin.

186
00:12:33,966 --> 00:12:36,688
Now put the six pieces back together.

187
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:38,232
A signed Ottoman map.

188
00:12:38,232 --> 00:12:39,777
About twenty sources.

189
00:12:39,777 --> 00:12:41,468
A corner from Columbus.

190
00:12:41,468 --> 00:12:43,086
A coast from Portugal.

191
00:12:43,086 --> 00:12:45,808
A southern edge bent to fit the hide.

192
00:12:45,808 --> 00:12:47,500
And no camera anywhere.

193
00:12:47,966 --> 00:12:50,429
What you are holding is not alien.

194
00:12:50,429 --> 00:12:52,241
It is naval intelligence.

195
00:12:52,241 --> 00:13:02,166
Captured Spanish and Portuguese charts, prisoners' testimony and classical geography, reduced to one scale by one man on the Dardanelles.

196
00:13:02,633 --> 00:13:07,208
McIntosh is clear: it is not the most accurate map of its century.

197
00:13:07,208 --> 00:13:08,872
Many later maps beat it.

198
00:13:08,872 --> 00:13:17,399
Its value is that it captures the world in the very years it was being redrawn, and keeps a Columbus map we otherwise lost.

199
00:13:17,866 --> 00:13:20,003
Time for the jigsaw scorecard.

200
00:13:20,003 --> 00:13:20,928
Seven claims.

201
00:13:20,928 --> 00:13:24,133
Each one either fits the box, or it does not.

202
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:29,208
Drawn by Piri Reis in Gallipoli, fifteen thirteen: evidence.

203
00:13:29,208 --> 00:13:33,049
Compiled from about twenty older charts: evidence.

204
00:13:33,049 --> 00:13:36,121
Preserves a lost Columbus map: evidence.

205
00:13:36,121 --> 00:13:42,266
Southern coast as bent South America and an imagined southern land: most likely.

206
00:13:42,733 --> 00:13:46,117
An ice-free Antarctic coastline: debunked.

207
00:13:46,117 --> 00:13:49,823
A photograph from space above Cairo: debunked.

208
00:13:49,823 --> 00:13:53,933
Aliens, or a lost Ice Age civilisation: not needed.

209
00:13:54,399 --> 00:13:55,257
The verdict.

210
00:13:55,257 --> 00:13:59,117
The Piri Reis map does not show Antarctica from space.

211
00:13:59,117 --> 00:14:08,266
It shows something rarer: an Ottoman admiral gathering his rivals' secrets, and fitting a whole new world onto one gazelle skin.

212
00:14:08,733 --> 00:14:15,423
In the early fifteen fifties, in his eighties, Piri Reis was executed after a court intrigue.

213
00:14:15,423 --> 00:14:22,833
His map has outlived him by nearly five centuries, and is now on UNESCO's Memory of the World register.

214
00:14:23,299 --> 00:14:28,546
So when someone tells you this map is impossible, ask them to read the margins.

215
00:14:28,546 --> 00:14:31,866
The man who drew it already told us how he did it.

216
00:14:32,333 --> 00:14:33,337
That's the case.

217
00:14:33,337 --> 00:14:35,281
Which piece surprised you most?

218
00:14:35,281 --> 00:14:40,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery taken apart.
